This is just what I did that worked for me. This is on Debian Trixie using Swaywm.

Installing:

    wget https://github.com/sezanzeb/input-remapper/releases/download/2.2.0/input-remapper-2.2.0.deb
    sudo apt install -f ./input-remapper-2.2.0.deb


Opening GUI:
3. sudo -E input-remapper-gtk

Starting Daemon:
6. sudo input-remapper-service
7. sudo input-remapper-control --command autoload --device "Keyboard name " --preset "presetname"

You can find the device name using sudo input-remapper-control --list-device and the preset name will be whatever you set when you made it in the GUI (or the name of the json file in ~/.config/input-remapper-2/presets/"Device name"/presetname.json)

Automatically load preset on sign in:
add at end of sway config
exec input-remapper-control --command autoload

If the preset doesn't automatically load once you boot into your wm see if it's enabled using systemctl is-enabled input-remapper. If it's not enabled then you can enable it using sudo systemctl enable input-remapper. If you're still having issues then using sudo systemctl status input-remapper.service right after rebooting may be able to shed some light.

This is a mishmash of many hours googling and just what got it working for me. The key step is running sudo -E input-remapper-gtk as the program seemingly does not work unless the GUI is opened at least once.
